concepts.body:
Express functions accept extra mappings beyond x and y:

- `color="continent"`: a categorical column creates one trace per value with distinct colors; a numeric column becomes a continuous color scale with a colorbar.
- `symbol=` and `line_dash=` for marker shapes and dash styles;
- `size="pop"` scales marker area (`size_max=` caps it);
- `facet_col="year"`, `facet_row=`, and `facet_col_wrap=3` split the data into a grid of panels sharing axes;
- `hover_name="country"` puts a bold title on the tooltip, and `hover_data=` adds columns, with format strings or `False` to hide one: `hover_data={"pop": ":,", "iso_alpha": False}`.

Control colors with `color_discrete_sequence=px.colors.qualitative.Safe`, `color_discrete_map={"Asia": "red"}` for exact assignments, or `color_continuous_scale="Viridis"` for numeric color. `category_orders={"day": ["Thu", "Fri", "Sat", "Sun"]}` fixes the order of categories in legends, axes and facets.

Because categorical color creates separate traces, clicking a legend entry hides that group, and double-clicking isolates it.

Facet titles default to `column=value`; tidy them with `fig.for_each_annotation(lambda a: a.update(text=a.text.split("=")[1]))`.

Too many facets or colors overwhelm the reader; a dozen panels or eight colors is a practical limit.

Plotly figures can carry their own controls, which work in exported HTML with no Python running.

**Updatemenus** add buttons or dropdowns. Each button calls a plotly.js method with arguments:

- `"restyle"` changes trace properties, for example visibility: `args=[{"visible": [True, False]}]`;
- `"relayout"` changes layout, such as a title or axis type;
- `"update"` does both; `"animate"` plays frames.

**Sliders** work the same way with steps instead of buttons.

For time series, `fig.update_xaxes(rangeslider_visible=True)` adds a miniature overview to drag, and `rangeselector=dict(buttons=[dict(count=1, label="1m", step="month", stepmode="backward"), dict(step="all")])` adds quick-range buttons.

To run **Python code** in response to interaction, you need a live kernel or server:

- `go.FigureWidget` in Jupyter (requires the `anywidget` package) supports `trace.on_click(callback)` and `on_selection`, and updates when you change its properties from Python;
- Dash callbacks (`clickData`, `selectedData`, `relayoutData`) in web apps;
- Streamlit's `st.plotly_chart(fig, on_select="rerun")` returns selected points.

Choose in-figure controls for simple view switching that must survive as a static file; choose widgets or apps when interaction requires computing new data.

In applications, treat a chart as a **pure function**: data and options in, `go.Figure` out, no `fig.show()` and no global state inside. Benefits: the same builder serves Dash, Streamlit, notebooks and HTML exports, and it is testable.

Because a figure is plain structured data, tests can assert on exactly what the user will see:

- the number and types of traces (`[t.type for t in fig.data]`);
- data values, after converting to lists: `list(fig.data[0].y)`;
- names, colors, axis titles, formats and hovertemplates;
- the absence of mistakes, such as no trace with an empty `x`.

Prefer these targeted assertions over comparing the full JSON: whole-figure snapshots break on every library upgrade because plotly.py adds or renames defaults. When you do snapshot, store `fig.to_plotly_json()` restricted to the keys you own.

Make builders deterministic. Sort inputs, fix category orders (`category_orders` or explicit lists), and avoid relying on DataFrame row order for colors, so the same data always yields the same figure.

Keep styling out of builders: apply a registered template instead, so tests on content stay stable when the visual design changes.

Visual regression (comparing rendered PNGs via Kaleido) catches layout problems, but is slow and needs Chrome; reserve it for a few key charts.
